// THE SEAM. This file is the one place where "what shape are the clouds" is answered, and everything
// downstream of it — the march, the lighting, the temporal accumulation, the composite — is written
// against `CloudFieldSample` and never against a producer.
//
// That matters because the programme has two producers by design (Docs/Clouds/ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4):
//
//   P  PROCEDURAL  computed from the component's parameters. Infinite, needs no content, and is the
//                  right tool for the far field, where a cloud's angular size is already below what the
//                  march can resolve and vertical structure is invisible in principle.
//   A  AUTHORED    sampled from voxel volumes an artist sculpted. Arbitrary silhouettes, tunnels, a cloud
//                  shaped like anything at all — the near field and the hero clouds.
//
// BOTH EXIST NOW. A joins at the bottom of this file as a `max` of the two profiles with the winner's
// detail type and density scale — one function, one level up from the `max` the four species already
// take — and NOTHING above this file changed when it landed: the march, the shadow map, the lighting,
// the temporal resolve and the composite are the text they were, because they were written against
// `CloudFieldSample` and never against a producer. The seam was written first in Э0 precisely so that
// the second producer would be a plug rather than a rewrite, and that is what it turned out to be.
//
// THE MEANING OF `Profile` IS LOAD-BEARING and is Nubis's, not a density: it is how deep inside the body
// of the cloud a point is, 0 at the surface and 1 in the core (deck p.85, where it is generated from a
// signed distance field for exactly this gradient). Six different calculations end up hanging off it —
// the silhouette, the frequency blend of the wispy noise, the frequency blend of the billowy noise, the
// multiple-scattering volume, the ambient term, and the early-out — which is why it is worth carrying a
// quantity that means "depth inside" rather than the density a naive implementation would store.
//
// HOW THE DATA ARRIVES. Through two macros, never through a sampler declared here:
//
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( int slot,                ->  vec4, the four channels of ONE OF THE LAYER'S FOUR
//                         vec3 texturePosition )       noise volumes, which is the EROSION and nothing
//                                                      else now. The slot is the winning species' —
//                                                      CloudFieldSample::NoiseSlot — because a type names
//                                                      its own volume and a layer of four types can name
//                                                      four. The march defines this as a four-way select
//                                                      over four samplers; a test defines it as a call
//                                                      into the generator with that slot's periods.
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING( vec3 uvw )           ->  vec4, the procedural MODELLING VOLUME — ONE CHANNEL
//                                                      PER SPECIES, in the order of the layer's slots,
//                                                      each of them that species' Dimensional Profile
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( vec3 uvw )            ->  vec4, four channels of the sculpted-body ATLAS
//
// The third arrives on the same terms as the first two, and the instance list it is addressed with — plus
// the atlas's slab count — comes from Common/CloudAuthored.glslh, which must have been included first.
//
// The march defines them as `texture()` fetches; the unit tests define the first as a call into the same
// CloudNoiseVolumeChannels that baked the volume and the second as a filtered read of the very volume
// Assets::BakeCloudProceduralVolume produced. This is the mechanism SkyScattering.glslh already uses for its
// LUT callbacks, and it exists because a sampler cannot appear in text that is also compiled as C++ —
// which is the only way the maths below gets a test at all. When the macros are undefined the structural
// half of this header still compiles, which is what lets a consumer include it for the types alone.
//
// Dialect rules as in Common/CloudNoise.glslh.

#ifndef CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH

// Requires Common/CloudNoise.glslh, Common/CloudGeometry.glslh and Common/CloudAuthored.glslh to have
// been included first. GLSL has no forward declarations, so include order is the caller's responsibility
// and is stated here rather than discovered as a compile error.

// HOW MANY KINDS OF CLOUD ONE SKY HOLDS AT ONCE. Four, and the number is the width of a texel rather than
// a preference: a species owns one CHANNEL of the profile table, which is Unreal's arrangement exactly
// (EpicDoc_CloudMaterial.md §2-3 fixes four types to R, G, B and A). Graphic::kCloudSpeciesSlots is the
// same constant on the C++ side. The LIBRARY on disk is not limited by this; a single LAYER is.
#define CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS 4

// What every producer returns and what the whole renderer consumes.
//
// THE LAST THREE ARE THE WINNING SPECIES', and they travel here rather than in the parameter block for a
// reason that only exists once a sky has more than one kind of cloud in it. What a cloud is MADE OF — how
// deeply its edge erodes, how much matter is in it, how opaque that matter is — is a property of the kind
// of cloud, so with four kinds in one sky it stops being a constant of the march and becomes a property of
// the SAMPLE. Carried from the species whose profile won the union, never blended: a blend of a
// stratocumulus and a congestus is a third thing that is neither, and the seam where two bodies meet is
// exactly where a floating average is visible as a smear of the wrong character.
struct CloudFieldSample
{
    float Profile;          // 0..1, depth inside the cloud body (see the header note)
    float DetailType;       // 0 = wispy, 1 = billowy — selects which erosion the up-rez applies
    float DensityScale;     // per-sample multiplier applied AFTER erosion (deck p.118)
    float DetailFactor;     // the winner's multiplier on the layer's erosion depth
    float ExtinctionFactor; // the winner's multiplier on the layer's extinction

    // WHICH NOISE VOLUME THE WINNER'S EDGE IS CUT FROM, 0..CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS-1. It travels with the
    // sample for the same reason the four above do, and the reason is sharper here than for any of them:
    // DetailCharacter and DetailFactor can only choose between the pairs of ONE volume and how deep to cut
    // with them, so the SCALE of an edge — the lattice period the noise was baked at — has no other way to
    // differ between two kinds of cloud in one sky. The shipped Cirrus is the type that needs it: it names
    // a volume twice as fine as the default, and until this field existed it got that volume only when it
    // happened to sit in the layer's first filled slot.
    int NoiseSlot;
};

// Everything the procedural producer needs, unpacked from the GPU block by Common/CloudParams.glslh.
// Distances are KILOMETRES, matching Common/CloudGeometry.glslh.
struct CloudFieldParams
{
    float DetailTileKm;     // period of the erosion field
    float DetailStrength;   // how deeply the erosion cuts into the profile, for the LAYER
    float DensityScale;     // the LAYER's density multiplier; the species' own is folded in per sample
    int   SpeciesCount;     // how many channels of the modelling volume are filled, 0..CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS

    // Per species k: x DetailCharacter, y DetailFactor, z DensityFactor, w ExtinctionFactor. Four numbers
    // that describe what that kind of cloud is made of, and all four are read.
    vec4 SpeciesEdge[CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS];

    // Per species k: which of the layer's bound noise volumes its edge is cut from. DEDUPLICATED by
    // Graphic::ResolveCloudNoiseVolumes, so a sky whose types name one volume between them — which is
    // eight of the nine shipped types — sends {0,0,0,0} and the fetch below is selected by a value that is
    // uniform across the wave. That is what makes this cost nothing where nothing was asked for.
    int SpeciesNoise[CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS];

    // WHERE THE MODELLING VOLUME IS, and it is two numbers rather than a matrix because the region is
    // axis-aligned by construction: the minimum corner of the region in world kilometres, and the
    // reciprocal of its side. Both are the CPU's — Assets::CloudProceduralRegionOriginKm decides the
    // corner and snaps it to the lump lattice, and the march only maps a position through it.
    vec2  RegionOriginKm;
    float InvRegionSizeKm;

    vec3 WindOffsetKm; // accumulated wind displacement, subtracted from the sample position

    // WHICH KIND OF RAY IS ASKING — CLOUD_RAY_VIEW or CLOUD_RAY_SHADOW, below.
    //
    // It rides here rather than being an argument of the five medium entry points because it is a
    // property of the MARCH and not of the sample: a whole quadrature is either integrating radiance
    // toward an eye or optical depth toward a light, and it never changes between two samples of one
    // loop. Stating that once per march is four assignments in this tree; stating it per sample would
    // have been the same fact repeated at eighteen call sites, and would have changed the signature of
    // every medium function — including the ones a graph never sees a shadow ray in.
    //
    // THE DEFAULT IS THE EXPENSIVE ONE, and that is deliberate. A march that forgets to declare itself
    // gets CLOUD_RAY_VIEW, so an authored medium runs its FULL body there: forgetting this costs the
    // author the saving they asked for and can never cost the frame its correctness. The other polarity
    // would have made a new consumer silently render a cheapened medium as if it were the real one.
    float ShadowRay;
};

// THE TWO RAY KINDS, and the boundary between them is what the answer IS, not where it points.
//
// CLOUD_RAY_VIEW marks a march that accumulates RADIANCE: the camera's march and the sky panorama's.
// CLOUD_RAY_SHADOW marks a march that accumulates OPTICAL DEPTH and hands back a transmittance — the sun
// quadrature in CloudLightOpticalDepth below, the cloud shadow map, and the sky-occlusion volume. Epic
// exposes the same axis as ShadowSampleDistance and for the same purpose: a medium may be authored
// cheaply where its only job is to attenuate, and expensively where it is seen.
//
// It is a float rather than a bool because it is an OUTPUT PIN of the graph's Cloud Sample node
// (O1_DESIGN §3.3) and the node graph's scalar type is float; a `mix( expensive, cheap, ShadowRay )` is
// then the whole idiom, with no branch to author.
#define CLOUD_RAY_VIEW 0.0f
#define CLOUD_RAY_SHADOW 1.0f

// There are deliberately NO SEEDS AND NO OCTAVE COUNTS here. Both are properties of the BAKE — they
// decide what is in the volume, not how it is read — so carrying them into the march would be four
// parameters that every sample copies and no sample uses. They live in the bake pass's push constant,
// which is the only place they are read.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The anisotropy of the EROSION octave. THREE NUMBERS, NOT ONE SCALAR.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// There were two triples here and there is one, because the other described the COVERAGE field's own
// anisotropy — and there is no coverage field any more. Where a cloud is and how tall it is are decided by
// the lumps of the modelling volume now (Engine/Assets/CloudProceduralVolume.hpp), and a lump's stretch is
// three radii of an ellipsoid rather than three frequencies of a noise: exact where this was a ratio, and
// authored per species by the lattice's own anisotropy.
//
// What survives is the triple that never described placement at all. Unreal's graph carries anisotropy on
// all three of its octaves (ShapeModel.md §11.2, coefficients at L1124), and the third one is INVERTED —
// its vertical coefficient is BELOW its horizontal one, so that octave is stretched UPWARD rather than
// flattened. Our erosion octave has no counterpart in the graph, so it takes the remaining one, and the
// fact that the remaining one is the inverted one is a small piece of luck: wisps that are taller than
// they are wide is what a torn cloud edge looks like.
//
// Y IS UP HERE AND Z IS UP IN UNREAL, so the middle number is Unreal's third.
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_X 1.0f
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Y 0.833f // 25 / 30 — BELOW one: this octave is stretched upward
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Z 1.333f // 40 / 30

// THE CEILING ON THE THREE MATERIAL FACTORS, and it is here because a `max(x, 0)` is only half a bound.
//
// Detail Factor, Density Factor and Extinction Factor all reach this file as a multiplier on a LAYER
// slider, and every one of those products is written `slider * factor`. A slider at zero is not an edge
// case — it is the position the artist is invited to use, and the whole of
// CloudFieldDensity.AtZeroStrengthTheProfileSurvivesUntouched exists to assert that turning the erosion
// off works. But `0 * (+inf)` is NaN, not zero, and a NaN density is the one value the march cannot
// survive: it propagates into the optical depth, from there into the transmittance, and shows as a black
// or fully transparent hole in the cloud with nothing in the log.
//
// A lower bound alone does not close it, because `max(+inf, 0)` is `+inf`. So the factors are bounded on
// BOTH sides at the point they enter a sample.
//
// WHY EIGHT AND NOT FOUR. Eight is the ceiling Assets::ValidateCloudTypeShape already enforces on the
// procedural side (Engine/Assets/CloudTypeData.cpp), so on that path this bound is the identity and can
// change no shipped pixel. It is four times the reflected Range of the authored side's own three factors
// (ECS::HeroCloudData) and four times the layer's Density Scale slider (Range(0, 2)), so it is the
// identity on every value the editor can produce as well.
//
// It is restated in the shader rather than trusted to those validators because only ONE of them exists.
// The reflected Range constrains a slider and nothing else: a scene file carrying a value outside it is
// deserialised verbatim (Core/Serialize/ComponentRegistry.cpp clamps nothing), survives the packer's
// `std::max(x, 0.0f)` unchanged, and arrives here. A shader must not depend on a CPU-side check it cannot
// see, and on the authored path there is no check to depend on.
#define CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR_MAX 8.0f

// One bound, applied wherever a factor is read out of a species row, an instance row or the layer block
// on its way into a product with a slider.
#define CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( x ) min( max( x, 0.0f ), CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR_MAX )

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE MODELLING VOLUME: what replaced the coverage threshold, and why it had to
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHAT STOOD HERE. A weighted blend of the noise volume's two Alligator channels, mapped through its own
// cumulative distribution so that `Coverage` addressed a fraction of sky, thresholded by a smoothstep, and
// multiplied by a two-dimensional table `f(height in the envelope, how deep inside the patch)`. Every one
// of those pieces was individually right, and the construction had a defect NONE of them could fix:
//
//     Alligator = best - second, and that field is ZERO wherever two feature points contribute equally.
//
// There is a wall of zeros between every pair of cells. Two lobes therefore cannot merge at any setting of
// any slider, and the sky comes out as a deck of separate cushions rather than as a convective mass. Three
// tasks measured it independently — T2 on the field, T0 on the profile, T3 on four independent placement
// fields — and all three worked around it.
//
// WHAT IS HERE INSTEAD is variant C of Docs/Clouds/ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §3 point 2, approved as decision
// D-1 and then substituted for the cheaper threshold: the profile is generated not as "coverage times a
// gradient" but as a SUM OF SMOOTHED VOLUMETRIC LUMPS JOINED BY AN EXPONENTIAL SMOOTH MINIMUM, and the
// profile IS the normalised distance field of that join. The smooth minimum has no wall of zeros; merging
// is its defining property rather than a setting.
//
// It is not evaluated here. Hundreds of lumps per region against ninety-five million field samples a frame
// is the wrong way round, so the join is baked into a camera-centric 3D texture whenever the parameters
// change or the region shifts — never per frame — and the march performs ONE FETCH. That fetch answers for
// all four species at once, where the arrangement it replaces cost one 2D fetch plus one 3D fetch per live
// species.
//
// THE HEIGHT-DEPENDENT DOMAIN WARP IS STILL NOT HERE, and the measurement that removed it stands: Unreal
// displaces its base octave's coordinates by a second noise on a height-dependent vector, because its
// placement pattern is a two-dimensional texture and the warp is the only three-dimensionality it has.
// Measured on Clouds_Demo at three elevations by two azimuths, the whole travel of that control moved the
// contrast by at most 0.018 with no consistent sign. Our field is three-dimensional by construction and is
// now MORE so, not less: a lump has three radii and an altitude.

// The procedural modelling volume's HEIGHT, which the march needs as a compile-time number to pull the
// vertical fetch in by half a texel. Assets::kCloudProceduralVolumeHeight is the same number on the C++
// side, and Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudField asserts the two statements agree — that suite compiles this
// header as C++, which is the only place the comparison can be made at all.
//
// ONE EXTENT AND NOT THREE. The first draft declared the width and the depth beside it "for symmetry",
// and neither was ever read: the horizontal mapping is `(world - origin) * invRegionSize`, which is in
// texture units already and never needs to know how many texels there are. A sabotage run caught it —
// setting the height to 64 left both suites GREEN, and looking at why turned up two dead macros as well
// as the missing assertion. A constant nobody reads is a setting nobody reads with extra steps
// (contract §1.3).
//
// PROCEDURAL AND NOT MODELLING IN THE NAME, because Common/CloudAuthored.glslh already spends
// CLOUD_MODELLING_VOLUME_* on the SCULPTED body's 128 x 64 x 128. Two volumes, two producers, two sets of
// extents — and the first draft of this file called both of them the same thing, which the shader compiler
// caught as a macro redefinition rather than as a silently wrong fetch.
#define CLOUD_PROCEDURAL_VOLUME_HEIGHT 32.0f

// WHERE A WORLD POSITION LANDS IN THE VOLUME.
//
// HORIZONTALLY IT IS NOT CLAMPED AND MUST NOT BE. The bake splats every lump at its wrapped positions too,
// so the volume is exactly periodic — and REPEAT sampling of a periodic volume is the DEGENERATE FAR PATH
// of §3 point 3: past the region the sky is the region again, at a distance where a cloud's angular size is
// already below what the march resolves (§4.5). Clamping instead would put a smear of the edge column
// across everything beyond 24 km.
//
// VERTICALLY IT IS CLAMPED TO THE TEXEL CENTRES, and that is not symmetry-breaking for its own sake: the
// volume spans the shell exactly, every sampler in this engine is REPEAT, and without the clamp a height
// fraction of 1 wraps to the bottom row — so the top of the layer would grow the bases of its own clouds
// back on top of itself. The same clamp, for the same reason, that the profile table needed.
vec3 CloudProceduralVolumeUvw( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 windPos )
{
    float halfV = 0.5f / CLOUD_PROCEDURAL_VOLUME_HEIGHT;

    return vec3( ( windPos.x - params.RegionOriginKm.x ) * params.InvRegionSizeKm,
                 clamp( heightFraction, halfV, 1.0f - halfV ),
                 ( windPos.z - params.RegionOriginKm.y ) * params.InvRegionSizeKm );
}

#if defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE ) && defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING ) && defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED )

// The procedural producer — ONE FETCH, four species.
//
// WHAT IT USED TO BE: a loop over the live species, each of them costing a 3D fetch of the noise volume at
// its own scale, a quantile map, a smoothstep threshold and a second fetch of the profile table, guarded by
// a cheap rejection that was itself a fetch. What it is now is a single trilinear read of the modelling
// volume, whose channel k is species k's Dimensional Profile — already three-dimensional, already fused,
// already normalised, because the bake did all of it (Engine/Assets/CloudProceduralVolume.hpp).
//
// WHY THE SPECIES ARE STILL JOINED BY `max` AND NOT BY THE SMOOTH MINIMUM. Inside a species the lumps fuse,
// because a convective mass is what a species IS; across species they must not. Decision D-14's target
// frame is a low stratocumulus deck with a congestus tower standing in it and partly behind it — two
// KINDS of cloud overlapping, each keeping its own character — and a smooth minimum there would grow a
// third body in the gap between them that is neither. `Profile` means "how deep inside a body of cloud this
// point is", so the deeper of two answers is the answer, and the order the species were listed in cannot
// reach the result.
//
// THE WINNER TAKES ALL FOUR MATERIAL NUMBERS and shares none of them. See the note on CloudFieldSample: a
// blend of a stratocumulus' and a congestus' detail character is a third character that is neither, and the
// seam where two bodies meet is exactly where a floating average shows as a smear of the wrong kind.
CloudFieldSample CloudSampleProceduralField( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudFieldSample result;
    result.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;
    // Slot 0 is the one index that is ALWAYS bound (Graphic::ResolveCloudNoiseVolumes fills the unused
    // descriptors with it), so the empty sample names a real image rather than a number the fetch would
    // fall through on. Nothing reads it: CloudSampleDensity returns at a profile of zero, above the fetch.
    result.NoiseSlot = 0;

    // THE WIND IS A SHIFT OF THE SAMPLE AND NOT OF THE VOLUME, which is what keeps the wind free: the
    // region's origin is chosen around the camera MINUS this same offset (Assets::
    // CloudProceduralRegionOriginKm, called with the shifted camera), so the point being asked about is
    // inside the region that was baked for it however far the wind has run.
    vec3 windPos = vec3( positionKm.x - params.WindOffsetKm.x, positionKm.y - params.WindOffsetKm.y,
                         positionKm.z - params.WindOffsetKm.z );

    vec4 volume = CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING( CloudProceduralVolumeUvw( params, heightFraction, windPos ) );

    int speciesCount = min( params.SpeciesCount, CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS );

    for ( int slot = 0; slot < speciesCount; ++slot )
    {
        float speciesProfile = clamp( volume[slot], 0.0f, 1.0f );
        if ( speciesProfile <= result.Profile )
            continue;

        vec4 edge = params.SpeciesEdge[slot];

        result.Profile          = speciesProfile;
        result.DetailType       = clamp( edge.x, 0.0f, 1.0f );
        result.DetailFactor     = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( edge.y );
        result.DensityScale     = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( params.DensityScale ) * CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( edge.z );
        result.ExtinctionFactor = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( edge.w );
        // THE FIFTH THING THE WINNER TAKES, and the one the union could not carry until now. Not blended,
        // for a sharper version of the reason the four above are not: a volume is not a number, and the
        // midpoint between two of them is not a third volume.
        result.NoiseSlot = params.SpeciesNoise[slot];
    }

    return result;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SLOT A — THE AUTHORED PRODUCER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What slot A answers with: a field sample AND how much of the procedural field this point's instances
// are suppressing. Two values because the cutout is not a property of the sample — it decides what the
// OTHER producer is allowed to contribute — and this dialect has no `out` parameters, deliberately.
struct CloudAuthoredResult
{
    CloudFieldSample Field;
    float            Cutout; // 0 = the procedural field stands, 1 = it is entirely suppressed here
};

// Sample every hero cloud whose bounds contain this point, and union them.
//
// THE COST IS THE WHOLE DESIGN OF THIS FUNCTION, because it is called once per march step AND once per
// SHADOW-RAY step, which is about thirty times more often again. Three gates in order of price:
//
//   1. THE COUNT. A scene with no hero clouds does not enter the loop at all. That is what makes the
//      producer cost zero when it is absent rather than almost zero (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4.4). The
//      frames measure it: five of the protocol's six points render BYTE FOR BYTE the file they rendered
//      before this producer existed, and the sixth moves 199 pixels of 980 480 by one 8-bit level —
//      which is not this loop but the recompiled SPIR-V scheduling the PROCEDURAL producer's floating
//      point differently, because the file it lives in changed. Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md §A0.
//   2. THE AXIS-ALIGNED BOX, six compares. It is a superset of the instance's real bounds and it is here
//      because it is four times cheaper than the map that follows.
//   3. THE EXACT BOX, after the map. A rotated instance's AABB contains up to 3.4x its volume, and a
//      point in that difference must not fetch: every sampler in this engine is REPEAT, so a coordinate
//      outside [0, 1] would fetch the far side of the body and grow a mirror of its own flank beside it.
//
// The union rule is the SPECIES' RULE, one level up (§4.3): profile means "how deep inside a body", so
// the deeper answer is the answer, and the winner's material numbers travel with it rather than being
// blended — a blend of two bodies' detail character is a third character that is neither, and the seam
// between them is exactly where a floating average shows as a smear.
CloudAuthoredResult CloudSampleAuthoredField( CloudFieldParams params, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudAuthoredResult result;
    result.Field.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.Field.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;
    result.Cutout                 = 0.0f;

    // A SCULPTED BODY IS ERODED BY SPECIES 0'S VOLUME, AND THAT IS UNCHANGED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. A hero
    // cloud is not a species — it has no `.decloudtype` and therefore names no volume — and the volume it
    // was cut from before this file could tell four apart was the layer's one volume, which was the first
    // filled slot's, which is species 0's. So this line is the old behaviour written down, and it is
    // written down HERE rather than left implicit because "0" would otherwise read as a placeholder.
    // Giving an instance its own volume is an authored field this task did not add: a knob nobody asked
    // for is a knob nobody tunes (PLAN_AUTHORED_CLOUDS.md).
    result.Field.NoiseSlot = params.SpeciesNoise[0];

    int count = CLOUD_AUTHORED_COUNT;

    // THE ABSENT CASE COSTS WHAT IT COST AT A0 AND NOT ONE INSTRUCTION MORE: one integer load and one
    // compare, per field sample, of which a frame takes on the order of 95 million. The slab count is
    // read AFTER this line and not beside the count above, so a sky with no hero clouds in it does not
    // load it — which is the difference between "zero when absent" holding and drifting by a load per
    // sample every time this producer gains a global (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4.4).
    if ( count == 0 )
        return result;

    int slabCount = CLOUD_AUTHORED_SLAB_COUNT;

    for ( int i = 0; i < count; ++i )
    {
        CloudAuthoredInstance instance = CLOUD_AUTHORED_INSTANCE( i );

        if ( !CloudAuthoredInBounds( instance, positionKm ) )
            continue;

        vec3 uvw = CloudAuthoredLocalUvw( instance, positionKm );
        if ( !CloudAuthoredInVolume( uvw ) )
            continue;

        // THE FETCH IS INTO THE ATLAS, and which body it lands in is the instance's own slab origin. That
        // is the whole of what phase A2 added to this loop: one multiply-add on the depth coordinate, so
        // eight DIFFERENT sculpted bodies cost the march exactly what one did.
        vec4 voxel =
             CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( CloudAuthoredAtlasUvw( uvw, instance.BoundsMax.w, slabCount ) );

        float strength = CloudAuthoredStrength( instance );

        // THE CUTOUT UNIONS ACROSS INSTANCES AND IS NOT TIED TO THE WINNER. Two hero clouds standing in
        // each other's boxes each suppress the procedural field over their own body, and the procedural
        // field has to be gone from BOTH — taking only the winner's would let a blob grow through the
        // one that lost by a hair.
        //
        // It is the volume's ENVELOPE channel and not the box, which is the difference between a hero
        // cloud that pushes the deck away from its own skin and one that cuts a rectangular hole in the
        // sky around itself. The envelope is the body dilated by the bake's margin, so it is above zero
        // wherever the profile is and extends past it: a tunnel through the body narrower than that
        // margin is inside the envelope and shows sky, which is what a tunnel is for.
        result.Cutout = max( result.Cutout, voxel.w * CloudAuthoredCutout( instance ) );

        float profile = voxel.x * strength;
        if ( profile <= result.Field.Profile )
            continue;

        result.Field.Profile      = profile;
        result.Field.DetailType   = clamp( voxel.y, 0.0f, 1.0f );
        result.Field.DetailFactor = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( instance.Row0.w );
        // The layer's density, this body's factor and the volume's own per-voxel scale, in that order —
        // the same three-level composition the procedural producer performs, so a Density Scale of 1
        // means the same thing on both sides of the seam.
        result.Field.DensityScale =
             CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( params.DensityScale ) * CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( instance.Row1.w ) * voxel.z;
        result.Field.ExtinctionFactor = CLOUD_MATERIAL_FACTOR( instance.Row2.w );
    }

    return result;
}

// THE SEAM ITSELF. One producer that unions up to four species, one that unions up to eight sculpted
// bodies, and the max of the two — the same `max`, one level up, which is what both unions below it were
// written to make obvious.
//
// THE AUTHORED SIDE GOES FIRST, and not because either order changes the union: `max` is commutative and
// Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored asserts it on the whole sample. It goes first because it decides
// whether the procedural side is computed AT ALL. Inside a hero cloud the cutout is 1, the procedural
// field is entirely suppressed there, and computing it would be a 3D fetch, a profile fetch and a
// quantile map thrown away — so the branch below is a saving in exactly the place a hero cloud makes the
// frame most expensive.
CloudFieldSample SampleCloudField( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudAuthoredResult authored = CloudSampleAuthoredField( params, positionKm );

    CloudFieldSample result;
    result.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;
    result.NoiseSlot        = 0; // always bound; nothing reads it at a profile of zero

    if ( authored.Cutout < 1.0f )
    {
        result = CloudSampleProceduralField( params, heightFraction, positionKm );

        // THE CUTOUT SCALES THE PROFILE RATHER THAN THRESHOLDING IT, so the procedural field fades out
        // over the envelope's own margin instead of ending at a surface. A hard test would put a visible
        // edge in the deck at the exact distance the envelope reaches, and that edge would be a shape
        // nobody authored — the bake's margin — drawn in somebody else's cloud.
        result.Profile = result.Profile * ( 1.0f - authored.Cutout );
    }

    if ( authored.Field.Profile > result.Profile )
        result = authored.Field;

    return result;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THE EROSION CANNOT PUT A SURFACE ON THIS CLOUD, AND WHAT WAS REFUSED — task Р9, 2026-08-28
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE OWNER'S COMPLAINT THAT THIS ANSWERS is that the lobes are smooth, rounded and uniform: cotton
// wool. Р0 got as far as two findings and could not join them — the erosion is "CONFIRMED as a ceiling,
// REFUTED as a knob" (Docs/Clouds/DIAGNOSIS_CARTOON.md §1 row 5) and our fine-scale energy over cloud
// pixels is about half the reference's, "and I could not name what limits it" (row 6, verdict OPEN).
// Р6 then refused the resolution explanation with numbers and said what was needed was "a mechanism
// that ADDS surface" (VolumetricCloudRenderer.cpp:36-42). This is what limits it: a ratio of two
// lengths, neither of which anything in the Detail block can move.
//
// THE SILHOUETTE IS A LINE INTEGRAL, NOT A SURFACE. A ray does not stop where the density becomes
// non-zero; it stops where it has accumulated unit optical depth. Measured on the shipped protocol sky
// by Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudField (TheEyeLooksThroughMoreCloudThanTheErosionVariesOver, which prints
// both numbers on every run):
//
//     the depth the eye looks through before the cloud is opaque   657 m
//     the distance over which the erosion field decorrelates       160 m      -> 4.11x
//
// Integrating 657 m of a field that decorrelates in 160 m is a LOW-PASS FILTER. The erosion arrives at
// the silhouette perfectly fine-grained — that was never the problem — and then four correlation lengths
// of it are averaged together before anything reaches the eye. What survives is its long-wavelength
// part, which is a cloud of a slightly different SIZE rather than a cloud with a surface. The erosion
// contributes 4.1 m of roughness at an 80 m lag on top of the 94.3 m the bare lumps already have: 4.3 %.
//
// THAT ONE RATIO PREDICTS FIVE INDEPENDENT REFUTATIONS, four of which were already in the tree as
// separate mysteries:
//
//   * DetailStrength over its whole range is worth 2.86/255 (Р0) — it scales an amplitude that is
//     averaged away afterwards.
//   * DetailTileSize is worth 1.29/255 (Р0), and the sweep is now understood rather than observed: over
//     tile 0.5 / 1 / 2 / 3 km the silhouette's roughness at a 50 m lag reads 60.7 / 61.0 / 59.8 / 58.7 m.
//     A coarser tile survives the averaging and is too coarse to read as detail; a finer one reads as
//     detail and does not survive. The two cancel, which is why six tasks found nothing here.
//
//     >>> CORRECTED 2026-08-31, AND THE CORRECTION IS ABOUT THE STATISTIC RATHER THAN THE MECHANISM.
//     >>> Р11 disputed both bullets above; the integrator re-measured them himself rather than picking
//     >>> a side. The MEAN figures reproduce — DetailTileSize across its full authored range, 0.2 km
//     >>> against 30 km, moves the frame by a mean of 0.97/255 at AZ 000 / EL 25, and the disputed
//     >>> 0.5-to-3 km slice by 0.91. But THE FRAMES ARE NOT SUBTLY DIFFERENT, they are obviously
//     >>> different: at 0.2 km every silhouette carries a fine granular speckle and the small lobe has a
//     >>> ragged rim; at 30 km the same lobes are glass-smooth and the rim is a clean ellipse. See
//     >>> Docs/Clouds/Shots/P11_DetailTileSize_0k2km_* and _30km_*.
//     >>>
//     >>> A MEAN OVER THE FRAME IS THE WRONG SUPPORT FOR THIS CONTROL. What it changes is a
//     >>> high-frequency texture confined to the silhouette: a small share of the pixels, a low
//     >>> amplitude on each, and decisive to the eye. Averaging over 736 804 pixels drowns exactly the
//     >>> thing being judged — the same error as a whole-frame delta, taken along the other axis.
//     >>>
//     >>> So the MECHANISM above stands and reproduces. The CONCLUSION drawn from it — that these two
//     >>> controls are inert and a candidate for deletion — does not, and it was drawn by the same note
//     >>> that warns two paragraphs later that the numbers say WHERE a change landed and only the frame
//     >>> says whether it is better. Р11's own counter-figures (126/255, 81/255) did not reproduce here
//     >>> either; the verdict is right, the number is not, and both were checked before either was used.
//   * MaxSteps 256 -> 512 is worth 0.92/255 (Р0) and the trace octave 1.3-3.4 % for 2.83x (Р6): both are
//     about SAMPLING, and this is about AVERAGING.
//   * AND THE MODELLING VOLUME IS NOT THE LIMIT EITHER, which nobody had measured. Doubling it to
//     512 x 32 x 512 — 93.75 m per voxel, 32.00 MiB, four times D-9's allowance for it — moves the
//     silhouette's roughness at an 80 m lag from 94.3 m to 95.8 m. ONE AND A HALF PER CENT for four
//     times the memory. The volume carries the silhouette and the noise carries the edge
//     (PLAN_AUTHORED_CLOUDS.md §2), and neither producer's resolution is what is missing.
//
// So no resolution anywhere in the subsystem is the constraint, and the phrase this programme has been
// reaching for — "invest in the composition of the noise at up-rez" (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §6) — is right
// in kind but cannot be spent on frequency or on amplitude. Both are averaged.
//
// WHAT WAS BUILT AND REFUSED. The one term the averaging does NOT destroy is the SHAPE OF THE CUT'S
// HISTOGRAM. The erosion's depth is proportional to the noise, and the noise's median sits at 0.5 by
// construction (Common/CloudNoise.glslh's CLOUD_ALLIGATOR_MEDIAN_SHAPE, which re-labels the Alligator's
// naturally skewed field so a slider reads the middle of it — that calibration is right and was not
// touched). The side effect is that almost every sample receives a MIDDLING cut, so the erosion delivers
// itself as a uniform 138.7 m dent, and a uniform dent is exactly what a 657 m average cannot destroy and
// the eye cannot see. A mean-preserving contrast about that median spends the same average cut as clefts
// instead, costs one multiply and one clamp, adds no fetch and no parameter, and measures well:
//
//     contrast          1.0     1.5     2.0     3.0     4.0     6.0
//     roughness added   4.9 m   7.3 m   9.4 m  13.8 m  15.4 m  17.7 m   (50 m lag, above the bare lumps)
//     columns lost      0.070   0.069   0.066   0.062   0.060   0.057   (it opens FEWER holes, not more)
//     mean travel     138.7 m           125.0 m 113.5 m                 (the census, at strength 0.65)
//
// 2.8x the surface for free, and it was still refused, for two reasons measured in that order.
//
//   1. THE FRAME SAYS THE SURFACE IT ADDS IS THE WRONG SURFACE. At contrast 3 the near field grows a
//      visible RETICULATED LATTICE — a honeycomb of polygonal cells with bright ridges between them,
//      which is the Alligator's own `best - second` cell structure showing through once the cut is deep
//      enough to resolve it. Docs/Clouds/Shots/P9_contrast3_el45_away.png against
//      P9_base_el45_away.png: 78.9 % of pixels differ, max 99/255, and what changed is a lattice. It is
//      unmistakable at a second angle, P9_contrast3_el65_lattice.png, so it is the noise and not one view.
//      (The repeat shot on this scene is 0 differing pixels of 980 480 and the same md5, so every pixel
//      figure in this note is the change and nothing else.)
//      It is a NEAR-FIELD failure and the two elevations bracket it, which is why the dome sweep and not
//      six points is what found it:
//
//                              contrast (tonal)   p05     p95     pixels   max
//        EL 45 away, a lobe    0.464 -> 0.373    0.274 -> 0.366  78.9 %    99   <- lattice, and WORSE
//        filling the frame                                                       on Р0's own ruler
//        EL 25 sunward         0.245 -> 0.244    0.465 -> 0.476  71.1 %    76   <- reads as granularity
//
//      So it does not merely fail to help at the near field, it takes 0.091 of tonal contrast OFF the
//      frame there — filling the deep blue gaps, because a concentrated cut opens fewer holes — and
//      contrast is Р0's ranking #1. THE STATISTICS ALL SAID YES: more roughness, fewer holes, less
//      shrinkage, no change in penetration. Only the frame said no, which is §1 of the verify skill in
//      one line.
//   2. AND IT BREAKS TWO CALIBRATED RELATIONS, both of which are themselves mis-stated. Recorded because
//      whoever takes this next needs them restated before ANY deep-cut mechanism can land:
//      * TheLumpsAspectAndTheErosionsStrengthAreOneCalibrationAndNotTwoNumbers requires the MEAN travel
//        to clear 1.05x the march's resolvable chord (131.25 m). Contrast 2 lands on 125.0 and contrast 3
//        on 113.5. But the mean travel is the UNIFORM component — the part this whole note shows produces
//        no surface at all — so the relation is pinning the quantity that does not matter: at contrast 3
//        it falls 18 % while the roughness that does matter rises 180 %.
//      * HowMuchASampleKeepsRisesWithHowDeepInsideTheBodyItIs asserts retention is monotone in the
//        profile "because the weight is (1 - Profile) and nothing else in the expression depends on it".
//        THAT JUSTIFICATION IS FALSE ABOUT THIS FILE and was false before Р9: both `wispy` and `billowy`
//        below are frequency blends DRIVEN BY THE PROFILE. The relation holds today with a margin of
//        exactly 0.000 — a tie, not a pass — and any contrast above ~1.25 breaks it.
//
// WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY MOVE IT, priced, so the next task does not re-derive any of this:
//
//   * SHORTEN THE 657 m. It is set by the extinction and the profile ramp. Sixteen times the extinction
//     shortens it to 235 m and buys 4.9 -> 14.1 m of roughness — the same 2.8x the contrast bought — at
//     the cost of every lighting number the programme has calibrated. Measured, not estimated.
//   * GIVE THE EROSION VOLUME FRACTAL DETAIL AT THE BAKE. The lattice in reason 1 is the whole finding
//     about this noise, and the field's own structure function says the same thing without the picture:
//     sampled on the silhouette it reads D(50 m) 0.167, D(100) 0.210, D(200) 0.218, D(400) 0.220,
//     D(800) 0.219 — it SATURATES BY 200 m, which is the signature of a field with exactly ONE scale in
//     it. Each channel is one Alligator octave, so a cut deep enough to matter can only expose its
//     cells. A channel that summed two or three octaves would have no single cell scale to expose and
//     would keep rising past 200 m, and the cut could then be driven hard. That is
//     Common/CloudNoise.glslh (CloudNoiseVolumeChannels) plus a re-bake of the two shipped `.dcnv`
//     volumes, and it is where ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §6's "invest in the composition of the noise at
//     up-rez" actually cashes out — at the BAKE, not at the read. Р9 did not build it: it changes what
//     is IN a shipped asset, which is a content decision rather than a shader one.
//
// AND ONE THING THE WHOLE-DOME SWEEP SAYS THAT NO SINGLE FRAME DOES. At the sheet's 1/4 scale the
// contrast build reads BETTER over most of the dome — broken edges instead of smooth ellipsoids at
// EL 25, 65 and 85 (Docs/Clouds/Shots/P9_dome_contrast3.png against P9_dome_base.png). The defect is
// only in the near field and only at full resolution. A six-point protocol that happened to miss
// EL 45 would have shipped this.
//
// The contrast itself is NOT left in the tree behind a constant of 1: a knob that moves nothing is a
// dead setting (contract §1.3). It is one multiply and one clamp on `detailField` below, and this note is
// what it costs to put it back.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PAIR — CONTRAST ON A FRACTAL CHANNEL — WAS BUILT AND REFUSED. Task Р13, 2026-09-01.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Р10 ended by saying the pair was worth a task where neither half alone was: applying this contrast to a
// FRACTAL erosion channel, "the lattice does not appear at all, and the EL 45 penalty falls from Р9's
// 0.091 to 0.010 — a factor of nine". Р13 built both halves, re-baked both shipped volumes, and swept the
// whole dome twice. It does not survive.
//
// THE BASELINE MOVED FIRST. Every figure below is on a base re-shot with Р12's sky-light occlusion volume
// ON (`SkyOcclusionVolume = true`, `AmbientOcclusionStrength = 1.0`, and Clouds_Protocol sets both
// explicitly), so Р9's and Р10's absolutes are not comparable with these and only the DELTAS are. The
// repeat shot is 0 differing pixels of 980 480 and the same md5; the first render in the fresh worktree
// differed from the settled one by 16 pixels at a max of 1/255 and was discarded, exactly as the protocol
// says — that discard is not a formality, it is the same 110-pixel ghost the verify skill records.
//
// THE LATTICE APPEARS ON THE FRACTAL CHANNEL TOO, which is the claim the task rested on and it is false.
// `Docs/Clouds/Shots/P13_pair_el45_lattice.png` against `P13_base_el45.png`: the near lobe carries the
// same polygonal web of bright cell walls Р9 photographed on the single octave. It is not subtle and it
// is not hidden by having three lattices instead of one — the coarse octave still carries most of the
// amplitude, and it is the coarse octave's cells that the deepened cut exposes.
//
// AND THE FACTOR OF NINE IS A CHANGE OF SUPPORT, NOT A CHANGE OF SKY. Measured on this baseline at
// AZ 000 / EL 45, one frame, two rectangles:
//
//     support                       base     pair    penalty
//     the near lobe (400x310 px)    0.480 -> 0.397    0.083   <- Р9 measured a LOBE and got 0.091
//     the whole frame               0.399 -> 0.383    0.016   <- Р10 measured a FRAME and got 0.010
//
// Both figures reproduce. They are the same defect measured over two different areas, and the ninefold
// improvement is the ratio between the two rectangles rather than between two skies. This is the error
// the §Р11 correction three screens up already names — "a mean over the frame is the wrong support for
// this control... averaging over 736 804 pixels drowns exactly the thing being judged" — applied to the
// other side of the same argument. Р10's numbers were right; the comparison between them was not.
//
// THE WHOLE DOME, forty tiles, base against pair, tonal contrast on Р0's ruler:
//
//     tiles where contrast FALLS        35 of 40
//     tiles where it rises               4 of 40, by at most 0.009
//     mean change                       -0.0064
//     worst single tile        AZ 090 / EL 25   0.350 -> 0.307   (-0.043)
//
// Mean luminance rises on 33 of 40 and the cloud fraction grows — a concentrated cut opens FEWER holes,
// so the deep blue gaps fill in. Saturation falls with it (0.133 -> 0.120 at EL 45). At the sheet's 1/4
// scale the pair again reads BETTER over most of the dome, exactly as it did for Р9; the defect is in the
// near field at full resolution, and only EL 45 and EL 25 at full size show it.
//
// FIVE CALIBRATED RELATIONS BREAK, and they were restated with the truth BEFORE the pair was built, which
// is the only reason it is possible to say which of them is the pair's fault:
//
//   1. TheEyeLooksThroughMoreCloudThanTheErosionVariesOver — AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT SETTLES IT. The
//      contrast HALVES the erosion's decorrelation length, 160 m -> at most 80 m (the instrument's own
//      smallest lag), so the eye now integrates 8.21 correlation lengths instead of 4.11 — through the
//      bound of 8 that this very note sets as the point past which "the up-rez cannot reach the
//      silhouette at all". The mechanism eats itself: it buys roughness by making the field finer, and
//      finer is the direction the 657 m integral already destroys. THE FRACTAL DOES NOT DO THIS — with
//      the contrast at 1 the decorrelation is still exactly 160 m. It is the clipping in the contrast,
//      which turns gradual transitions into steps.
//   2. TheLumpsAspectAndTheErosionsStrengthAreOneCalibrationAndNotTwoNumbers — mean travel 138.7 m ->
//      113.9 m, 0.91x the march chord against a floor of 1.05x. Known and priced: the strength would
//      have to go 0.65 -> about 0.85 to pay for it, which is a shipped component default and four scenes.
//   3. TheShippedStrengthMovesTheSurfaceTheEyeSeesWithoutEatingTheBody — the same quantity, older floor.
//   4. HowMuchASampleKeepsRisesWithHowDeepInsideTheBodyItIs — worst margin -0.736 against a base of
//      exactly 0.000, and the comparisons where the cut RISES with the profile while the density is still
//      live go from 1 to 42. A sample 5 % deeper inside the body keeps 74 % less of its density: the
//      erosion is eating the CORE, which is the clouds-versus-fog defect the (1 - Profile) weight exists
//      to prevent.
//   5. TheCoreKeepsItsDensityAndTheEdgeLosesMostOfItAtFullErosionStrength — same cause.
//
// AND THERE IS NO CONTRAST THAT CLEARS THEM. 1.25 — Р9's own estimate of the break point, confirmed to
// the decimal — already fails 1, 4 and 5 (worst margin -0.0017, decorrelation already at 80 m). The
// correlation collapse is not gradual: the clipping starts as soon as the histogram is widened at all.
// So the legal range of this lever is (1.0, 1.25), where it buys 4.1 m -> under 7.0 m of roughness on a
// 94.3 m silhouette, and even that band fails the relation that matters most.
//
// WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER, so the next task does not re-derive it. Nothing in the composition of the
// noise will, and that is now measured from three directions rather than argued: amplitude (Р9),
// frequency (Р9), where the energy sits (Р10), octave count (Р13) and histogram shape (Р9, Р13) have all
// been spent. The binding constraint is the 657 m the eye integrates, and only its own two terms move it
// — the extinction and the profile ramp. Р9 priced that: sixteen times the extinction shortens it to
// 235 m and buys the same 2.8x of roughness, at the cost of every lighting number the programme has
// calibrated. That is the next task, and it is a lighting task, not a noise one.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE EXTINCTION WAS TAKEN AS THAT NEXT TASK AND MEASURED TO THE FRAME. IT DOES NOT PAY. Task Р18,
// 2026-09-03.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE SWEEP THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE ASKS FOR, taken with this file's own instrument — Desert/Tests/Engine/
// CloudField's TheEyeLooksThroughMoreCloudThanTheErosionVariesOver, run once per extinction:
//
//     extinction /km        8      16      24      32      45      64
//     penetration m       657     508     444     400     358     319
//     vs decorrelation   4.11x   3.18x   2.77x   2.50x   2.24x   1.99x
//     erosion adds m      4.1     6.7     6.1     8.0     9.1    11.3    (80 m lag)
//     bare lumps m       94.3    93.1    94.2    93.2    92.7    90.6
//
// TWO CORRECTIONS TO Р9'S PRICE, and they point in opposite directions. The roughness is CHEAPER than
// estimated — 2.76x arrives at eight times the extinction, not sixteen — and the penetration is far more
// STUBBORN than 1/sigma: eight times the extinction shortens it 2.06x, not eight, because a ray meets
// the cloud on the profile's ramp and a stronger medium merely stops a little earlier on the same slope.
//
// AND THE PRIZE IS FIVE METRES, WHICH IS THE WHOLE FINDING. The erosion's contribution goes 4.1 m ->
// 9.1 m at an 80 m lag, on a silhouette whose bare lumps already carry 92.7 m: 4.4 % of the silhouette
// becomes 9.8 % of it. The ratio doubles and the ABSOLUTE stays negligible, because the 657 m never
// bound the silhouette — it bound the EROSION's share of a silhouette the modelling volume owns.
//
// MEASURED ON THE FRAME, Clouds_Showcase from the owner's own camera (0,200,0), six points, 90 frames,
// noise floor zero (an explicit ExtinctionScale of 8 reproduced the default byte for byte at all six).
// Tonal contrast on Р0's ruler, Tools/ImageStat:
//
//     point            8 /km   16 /km   45 /km
//     zenith  -Z       0.238    0.234    0.242
//     zenith  +Z       0.216    0.193    0.181   <- the WRONG way, and the largest move of the six
//     mid     -Z       0.109    0.116    0.119
//     mid     +Z       0.110    0.114    0.115
//     horizon -Z       0.716    0.713    0.710
//     horizon +Z       0.742    0.741    0.739
//
// Five and a half times the extinction moves five of six points by less than 0.01 and the sixth by
// -0.035. Looking at the frames rather than the numbers says the same thing in the other direction: the
// zenith's SILHOUETTE does gain a visible crenellation, and the BODY behind it is exactly as flat as it
// was and slightly greyer. The complaint is about the body.
//
// WHY THE BODY DOES NOT MOVE, and this is where the chain that led here actually breaks. TWO KNOCKOUTS
// ON THE SAME FRAME (mid -Z, Clouds_Showcase, the owner's camera), each one field of the component:
//
//     shipped                                    mean 0.736   contrast 0.109
//     AmbientScale deleted                       mean 0.391   contrast 0.519   <- 4.8x
//     MultiScatterOctaves 3 -> 1                 mean 0.726   contrast 0.117
//
// The second row removes the sky's ambient. The third removes THE WHOLE MULTIPLE-SCATTERING SERIES and
// leaves single scattering alone. The ambient outweighs the entire series by about forty to one on Р0's
// own ruler — so on the near field the approximation this task was about is barely participating, and
// its extinction still less. The body is flat because it is lit by one full-sphere mean radiance through
// one scalar occlusion per sample, which is a field with almost no structure in it.
//
// That reproduces D-25's knockout on the owner's scene, AFTER Р7's composition fix and Р12's occlusion
// volume. Docs/Clouds/Shots/P18_showcase_mid_shipped.png against P18_showcase_mid_no_ambient.png.
//
// WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER. Not the extinction and not the noise. The silhouette's roughness is
// 92.7 m of LUMPS and 4.1 m of erosion, so a rougher silhouette is a question for
// Assets::BakeCloudProceduralVolume and a content decision. The flat near-field BODY is the ambient
// term, forty times the weight of everything the octaves do there.
//
// AND ONE QUALIFICATION THAT DOES NOT OVERTURN THE REFUSAL. On BROKEN cumulus — the same sky at coverage
// 0.35 instead of 0.78 — raising the extinction to 45 together with the occlusion the reference says
// belongs there (MultiScatterOcclusion 0.4847; see VolumetricCloudComponent.hpp) DOES visibly model the
// lobes: a shaded underside and a crenellated top where there was a smooth ellipse
// (Docs/Clouds/Shots/P18_broken_mid_ext08.png against P18_broken_mid_ext45_occ48.png). On the ruler it
// is a draw across six points — +0.058 / -0.072 / -0.002 / -0.005 / +0.031 / +0.031 — and the -0.072 is
// the sunward zenith, where the number falls because a thick cloud now correctly HIDES the sun whose
// disc used to be the p95. That is a different sky from the one the complaint was made about, and moving
// the default re-authorises three dozen scenes, so it is the owner's call and not this task's.

// THE MEDIUM IS AUTHORED, so it is not written here any more — it is INCLUDED, and a cloud material may
// substitute its own bytes for that one file at compile time (Engine/Core/ShaderCompiler/
// ShaderVariant.hpp, Docs/Clouds/O1_DESIGN.md §10.3). The density chain that stood in these lines moved
// into the file below UNCHANGED and is still what every program gets when nothing authors anything.
//
// THE INCLUDE SITS HERE, INSIDE THE PRODUCER GUARDS, and that is load-bearing: the medium is written
// against CloudFieldParams, CloudFieldSample and CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE, all of which exist only after the
// block above. It is also why all FOUR programs that sample the field receive the same medium without
// any of them naming it — they include this header, and this header includes the medium.
#include <Generated/CloudMedium.glslh>

// Optical depth from a sample toward the sun.
//
// IT LIVES HERE, IN THE HEADER, BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO MARCHES NOW. It was written inside
// Programs/Clouds/CloudRaymarch.shader while the eye was the only thing that lit a cloud; the sky's
// environment bake (Programs/Compute/BakeProceduralSky.shader) lights the same field for the panorama,
// and a copy of this quadrature beside the original is precisely the mirror this project has already paid
// for once — a C++ mirror of a gradient that drifted from the shader's own formula and turned every cloud
// grey. One text, two callers, and the seam's C++ tests can drive it.
//
// The samples are placed on a SQUARED distribution — Unreal's arrangement, and the reason is physical
// rather than aesthetic: transmittance is dominated by the material immediately around the sample, so
// uniform spacing spends most of its samples where the answer has already converged. Each segment's
// contribution is weighted by its own length, so the sum is a genuine integral and not a biased average.
//
// WHAT THAT DISTRIBUTION COSTS, and the defect it hid for a phase: the FIRST segment is
// @p marchKm / sampleCount^2 — quadratic in the count, not linear. The near field is therefore the one
// thing that does NOT survive lengthening the ray. At 500 m and six samples the first step was 13.9 m; the
// ray was then taken to 15 km for parity with Unreal's ShadowTracingDistance and the count was left at
// six, making it 417 m — a first step that walks straight out of the cloud the sample sits in and reports
// it transparent to the sun. Rendered, that was a sunward zenith highlight 4.4x the converged answer in
// linear radiance. Convergence needs about thirty samples on THIS quadrature; see
// ECS::kCloudLightMarchMaxSamples and Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md section OE-FIX. Anyone shortening
// @p marchKm again may lower the count with it, and by the square.
float CloudLightOpticalDepth( CloudLayer layer, CloudFieldParams params, vec3 positionKm, vec3 toSun,
                              float marchKm, int sampleCount, float extinctionPerKm )
{
    // THIS IS THE SHADOW RAY, and it is the only one of the four that is not a whole program.
    //
    // @p params arrives as a COPY (GLSL parameters are `in` by default), so flipping the flag here marks
    // every medium sample this quadrature takes and nothing else — the view march that called it keeps
    // its own params untouched on the next iteration. That is the whole reason the flag lives on the
    // struct: the two marches of CloudRaymarch.shader and of BakeProceduralSky.shader are nested, so no
    // per-program constant could have separated them.
    params.ShadowRay = CLOUD_RAY_SHADOW;

    float accumulated = 0.0f;
    float previous    = 0.0f;
    float invCount    = 1.0f / float( sampleCount );

    for ( int i = 1; i <= sampleCount; ++i )
    {
        float normalized = float( i ) * invCount;
        float current    = normalized * normalized;
        float segment    = current - previous;

        vec3 samplePos = positionKm + toSun * ( marchKm * ( previous + segment * 0.5f ) );

        // Leaving the shell ends the march. Without this the samples keep accumulating against a height
        // fraction clamped to 1, which draws a shadow from cloud that is not there.
        float radius = length( samplePos );
        if ( radius > layer.TopRadiusKm || radius < layer.BottomRadiusKm )
            break;

        float heightFraction = CloudHeightFraction( layer, samplePos );
        vec3  fieldPos       = vec3( samplePos.x, length( samplePos ) - layer.BottomRadiusKm, samplePos.z );

        CloudFieldSample field   = SampleCloudField( params, heightFraction, fieldPos );
        float            density = CloudSampleDensity( params, field, fieldPos );

        // THE EXTINCTION IS THE SAMPLE'S, NOT THE LAYER'S, and it has to be read here as well as in the
        // view march. A shadow ray from a congestus tower passes through the stratocumulus deck beneath
        // it: with one extinction for the whole shell the deck would shade the tower as if it were made of
        // the tower's own material, and the ice of a cirrus at a quarter of a cumulus' opacity would cast
        // a cumulus' shadow.
        accumulated += density * extinctionPerKm * CloudSampleExtinctionFactor( params, field, fieldPos ) *
                       segment;
        previous = current;
    }

    // The weights above are fractions of the march, so the whole length scales the sum exactly once, here.
    return accumulated * marchKm;
}

#endif // CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE && CLOUD_SAMPLE_MODELLING && CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED

#endif // CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH
